  "type": "multipleCorrect",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "With respect to hypochlorite, chlorate and perchlorate ions, choose the correct statement(s).",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "LlvQh",
      "text": "The hypochlorite ion is the strongest conjugate base.",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "WdVJC",
      "text": "The molecular shape of only chlorate ion is influenced by the lone pair of electrons of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cl</mi></math>.",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "NbJ0M",
      "text": "The hypochlorite and chlorate ions disproportionate to give rise to identical set of ions.",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "kg3zC",
      "text": "The hypochlorite ion oxidizes the sulfite ion.",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>)</mo></math> since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HOCl</mi></math> is weakest oxyacid of chlorine. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ClO</mi></math> - become strongest conjugate base.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>)</mo></math>  </p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/51cb189b-a0eb-45c2-a615-0f7781047cda-image.png\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>)</mo></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>ClO</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>⟶</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ClO</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>27</mn></msup></math><br/>In hot solution rate of disproportionation is fairly rapid.<br/><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msubsup><mi>ClO</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo>⟶</mo><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><msubsup><mi>ClO</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo> </mo><mi>Keq</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>29</mn></msup></math> It is thermodynamically possible but kinetically, it is very slow reaction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>)</mo></math> Lower oxyacids are good oxidizing agents.<br/><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>SO</mi><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>Cl</mi><mo>⟶</mo><msubsup><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
